cartridge "BEETHOVEN MEDLEY/PRESENTS C. REGENA'S/1983"

' BEETHOVEN MEDLEY (historically titled LUDWIG) by C. Regena
' "Programming The TI" column, COMPUTE! magazine, Issue 41, October 1983,
' pages 226-228. All credit belongs to C. Regena.
'
' Historical context (summary of the original column):
' The program shows how CALL SOUND statements build a medley of familiar
' Beethoven pieces while graphics statements, slipped between the notes,
' draw a picture. A quarter note lasts 400 ms for "Ode to Joy" from the
' Ninth Symphony, where a repeated bass note sounds tied under the melody;
' an eighth note lasts 200 ms for the "Ecossaises", whose repeated phrase
' and two endings are subroutines; "Fur Elise" repeats its common phrase
' with GOSUB inside a FOR-NEXT loop; the finale, from the second movement
' of the Fifth Symphony, uses an andante eighth note of 800 ms, a dotted
' sixteenth U = 3/4 T and a thirty-second T/4, while a musical-note
' character is placed on the screen. The picture then holds.
'
' ColecoVision port in Amy by Amy Bienvenu, 2026.
' The listing was executed by a script that recorded when every XB
' statement runs: each CALL SOUND starts when the previous one ends, and the
' graphics that follow run at that moment. The three voices became Coleco
' BIOS sound streams (TI duration in ms x 60 / 1000 = frames, TI volume / 2
' = attenuation), and the drawing below waits on the frame counter, so the
' picture stays in step with the music.
' Adaptation liberties: picture tiles moved off the ASCII range (char + 64);
' one star moved out of the CRT overscan; the title letter left beside the
' trunk is cleared; an editorial page at power-on; fire replays the medley.
' TI rows/columns are 1-based; Amy X,Y are 0-based (X = col - 1, Y = row - 1).

u16 StartFrame = 0
u16 Target = 0
u16 Elapsed = 0
u8 NoteTick = 0
u8 NotePhase = 0
u8 BloomTick = 0
bool BloomSway = false
u8 StarTick = 0
bool StarGlow = false
bool StarsLit = false

  text screen
  nmi off
  set sound table LudwigSoundTable areas 3
  screen on
  EditorialPage

